atorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j$ and $r(t)={e}^{2t}\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)\\,i+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x={e}^{2t}$ and $y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇\\,f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}+1)\\,i+{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{ln}⁡{e}^{2t}\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{2t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)(2t+1)\\,i+2t{e}^{2t}\\,j)\\cdot (2{e}^{2t}\\,i+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+4t{e}^{2t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{2t}[(2t+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2t\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=0,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =2{e}^{2(0)}((2(0)+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0))+2(0)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2(0)))=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-37109/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-37109"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" :answer/id #uuid "072b3a2c-51e1-5068-a35c-fc556cf158d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" :answer/id #uuid "fd055a1c-7f6d-5d7c-ab67-b438eb5a55b6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2e" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "f0e8127e-6ad1-573d-a390-49a62781bbf8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection/d1334c45e7c70c1f4ad9b6ae7172d3ce4d30a01c47cddf7483238d60acf646d5" :answer/id #uuid "430864e5-9062-561d-96d7-0d8c8e0d3ef8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{e}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "f100b71c-7f52-5f77-a535-b6f14a0af49f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f318e820-78e3-5493-ae3a-fbb0bac7bbfe" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167498/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer-field/choices ["q-167498/selection/a2301b8122c312044fb5dc0b0c60c599b4f2f7f4ffcc198c9893873ab9165c9d" "q-167498/selection/2a28a53216361474017108aa9cf0fffd0fb392524d53aeb00b78355e4fa703c6" "q-167498/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-167498/selection/d1334c45e7c70c1f4ad9b6ae7172d3ce4d30a01c47cddf7483238d60acf646d5" "q-167498/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167498" :question/id #uuid "fc94ecdf-931f-5639-b669-8ab4e18bda27" :question/math-academy-id "q-167498" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=-1$ given that $∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\text{ }j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2x{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,i+{e}^{{x}^{2}+y}\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=t+{cos}^{2}⁡t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,i+{e}^{{sin}^{2}⁡t+t+{cos}^{2}⁡t}\\,j \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t+{cos}^{2}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t+1}\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}\\cdot (1-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{e}^{t+1}-2{e}^{t+1}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & ={e}^{t+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=-1,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(-1))] & ={e}^{-1+1}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167498/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167498"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "29cca9ae-6ab3-5b82-bc23-4ce58ea5024e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" :answer/id #uuid "8bf4deed-8f59-5239-96ea-182a6719f2fa" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection/63079cc748dc757e7a8e5256fd2c9d955e6cf36193b2548b0f6e7b6b72fbf55c" :answer/id #uuid "e9d82a55-ed59-5923-9653-4ef166d7b921" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-2\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection/8edf36f0d0c04b8e0e3f881a66fab0f1901a06368d5ca1f6d437f77714de6bc5" :answer/id #uuid "59e2a926-0a66-5a92-8c25-d3504b690400" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\pi }{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951" :answer/id #uuid "a8e10773-0c9b-5de7-ac65-01fafc4d6b56" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\pi " :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f2381b07-41d4-5fd1-97be-0f1352b725ae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167497/selection/8edf36f0d0c04b8e0e3f881a66fab0f1901a06368d5ca1f6d437f77714de6bc5" :answer-field/choices ["q-167497/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" "q-167497/selection/681a4b64a7bb464b7f554c4e26477e3547d3c60cd353fce5dda4ae108b1c4fa5" "q-167497/selection/63079cc748dc757e7a8e5256fd2c9d955e6cf36193b2548b0f6e7b6b72fbf55c" "q-167497/selection/8edf36f0d0c04b8e0e3f881a66fab0f1901a06368d5ca1f6d437f77714de6bc5" "q-167497/selection/986e06f062dcc0ad37fbb185f7e0d56b0fd8e74b571035e7dc0af3d06af89951"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167497" :question/id #uuid "2f69d68e-97b8-5bd1-b02a-2d2d412e0b54" :question/math-academy-id "q-167497" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\pi $ given that $∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\sqrt{t}\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=2xy\\operatorname{cos}⁡({x}^{2})\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2})\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=\\frac{t}{2}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+\\frac{1}{2}j) \\\\  & ={t}^{3/2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\pi ,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =\\frac{\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi (-1)+0}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-167497/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167497"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-94872"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j$ and $r(t)=\\frac{t}{2}\\,i+t\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,i+x\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{t}{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\,i+j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{2}))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "85cc2508-3aa1-5f07-a45a-bce4dc8d7b0f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "22c8ecdf-bb43-5137-bb45-6e0438cf704b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection/ca8e151443ec19a809f4f9475bcf6dbe7123843cddb7905322c6bc63e3ecda1a" :answer/id #uuid "ead8f959-b586-5e21-ba4a-a2c2c38f8229" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection/7feb5e0f6c76b0c71bdaa9bf37faffefb410886c54e255ae058843b3eea76fac" :answer/id #uuid "c1bae446-624b-5d51-812d-bbffa6ee4f4d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{\\pi }}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer/id #uuid "e39385dd-7b02-54b2-b05b-0dd98a97b0da" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3b70e1f5-ccd3-5c06-97b5-8016b89cadb8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94878/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473" :answer-field/choices ["q-94878/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-94878/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-94878/selection/ca8e151443ec19a809f4f9475bcf6dbe7123843cddb7905322c6bc63e3ecda1a" "q-94878/selection/7feb5e0f6c76b0c71bdaa9bf37faffefb410886c54e255ae058843b3eea76fac" "q-94878/selection/ae9425c9e204310ed598f729a242e0b42a2acbbf125bff30bc2080e10b8cf473"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94878" :question/id #uuid "a71f1849-5073-5a73-9b09-d27bdebee4f8" :question/math-academy-id "q-94878" :question/problem "Evaluate $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$ given that $∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j$ and $r(t)=2\\sqrt{t}\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\,j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=-y\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,i-x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(xy)\\,j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=2\\sqrt{t}$ and $y=-2\\sqrt{t}$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =-(-2\\sqrt{t})\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,i-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡[(2\\sqrt{t})(-2\\sqrt{t})]\\,j \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{t})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2\\sqrt{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,i+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\,j)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\,i+(-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}})i) \\\\  & =(-2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t))\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}+2\\sqrt{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t)-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{8},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{8}))] & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94878/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "cc017b0d-6418-570a-9a5d-57572e4fe70a"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94878"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" :answer/id #uuid "07e8c0fe-59e5-5f3e-9c62-7a561b7670f2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" :answer/id #uuid "95c8bd37-119c-58c9-a8b8-486d0411c8a6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection/f3f015f7bf164eadf2b2d92252a28c37b91b15d299981e3564221776c934dc48" :answer/id #uuid "a4e9c770-8ae7-53b7-b10e-331839c0ad1a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection/fcc08b5493ae6f7b4bc0aad6b1791b974d36d4679ce4f932434b29e95cebea06" :answer/id #uuid "1d76ebea-da83-54ae-98e3-4268ca1d990b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer/id #uuid "42307b3f-6914-5af8-971c-c3a5e3e1fd45" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "22b7fffa-5606-50a3-b01d-61fd102ce45c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-167499/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833" :answer-field/choices ["q-167499/selection/ad06fa8bb7e77edd4098e00237718855c96ba589745d004ae1aed5be17e6549c" "q-167499/selection/b7e1c893089e9c7b8066a199c43cdff517fe004f1db62d878596274884ad36ec" "q-167499/selection/f3f015f7bf164eadf2b2d92252a28c37b91b15d299981e3564221776c934dc48" "q-167499/selection/fcc08b5493ae6f7b4bc0aad6b1791b974d36d4679ce4f932434b29e95cebea06" "q-167499/selection/d027d90726f5bdbf1525aade1b6bde5c85e2082f2b792dfacee399fcc90e9833"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-167499" :question/id #uuid "ee672695-2847-53b1-a5ce-9d071b0d3c31" :question/math-academy-id "q-167499" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)=\\frac{x}{y}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\sqrt{t}⟩$ at the point $t=9.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\frac{x}{y}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\frac{x}{y})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{y},-\\frac{x}{{y}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=2t$ and $y=\\sqrt{t}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}},-2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}}⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}\\cdot 2-2\\cdot \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{t}}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=9$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(9))] & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{9}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-167499/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-167499"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer/id #uuid "19a87405-8140-5cd0-8a40-e5bb95ff6976" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "9" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" :answer/id #uuid "2d08b5c3-3235-5f80-b1f0-7b298f31a9e2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-7" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" :answer/id #uuid "a7eff3d1-6dd0-5c7b-b5cb-da56c21ada91" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "10" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" :answer/id #uuid "748984f7-d5e4-5df8-bd07-81571d9fb155" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-12" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa" :answer/id #uuid "6805d2ef-06c0-505d-8d59-6c5aa0d96918" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "6" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "51ca6068-4300-5c23-b15f-253600f9d0a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94888/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" :answer-field/choices ["q-94888/selection/303632d9519e4b7e53f9af56aad03bb866b8b5ede58a0b8b2cb848d575460c53" "q-94888/selection/25666b67a56594dd5245ec05e89d883db07d04362ab51dbc90c39ad20f7f9142" "q-94888/selection/56019ff80d11d678dbd5eca83c729f420f1b6a8ad4259032846618d6b48aa0bc" "q-94888/selection/f509030681214332b5989836fe0af4b2eb5858d193e689fd8441dff5cd45ff45" "q-94888/selection/a0e0e68f25b8b46aafef3b74aff7beff7fddc6de5bf36921680b04c7b0e6ffaa"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94888" :question/id #uuid "b9ab91e5-238b-5357-aa2a-bdeda8f76005" :question/math-academy-id "q-94888" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t,4,\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩$ at the point $t=2.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3}),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(x{z}^{2}+{y}^{3})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{z}^{2},3{y}^{2},2xz⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t,$ $y=4$ and $z=\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3})^{2},3(4)^{2},2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2}-3,48,2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}}⟩ \\\\  & =({t}^{2}-3)\\cdot 1+48\\cdot 0+2t\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}\\cdot \\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}-3}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}-3+0+2{t}^{2} \\\\  & =3{t}^{2}-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=2$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(2))] & =3(2)^{2}-3 \\\\  & =12-3 \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94888/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94888"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection/5e4b702325fa059681c737205b6be8268024df0566e8c210d5cce8990946d2e3" :answer/id #uuid "bded140d-5d0d-553a-b46e-5e6d2f4df7f7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" :answer/id #uuid "ef15fbd5-d7f4-588c-9a10-f8ed8980ea3c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection/2f887b6512b3b5fd774a1be904ebc1f0d651dd97ab64b723ce7361d39ea72769" :answer/id #uuid "52576b11-9a35-5c96-b041-3ec847b37b08" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}-2}{2}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" :answer/id #uuid "f055bb4f-ba18-58b0-8615-5d3b010d10cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection/2928cadc70d5b702972833091abf1ef12b1231b3d41635f0fa3e5d01b4fa7d3c" :answer/id #uuid "d078abf5-534a-54bf-950e-d5a516631c76" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "c8d83ee0-bd6c-51a1-b4e9-7bc5008f7e99" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-36227/selection/5e4b702325fa059681c737205b6be8268024df0566e8c210d5cce8990946d2e3" :answer-field/choices ["q-36227/selection/5e4b702325fa059681c737205b6be8268024df0566e8c210d5cce8990946d2e3" "q-36227/selection/d971375f57c56d8a55a09f2b892e108787b055ceeadf005d7beed73e388cc467" "q-36227/selection/2f887b6512b3b5fd774a1be904ebc1f0d651dd97ab64b723ce7361d39ea72769" "q-36227/selection/d427b944e26709d5f365fc6b5711be004a0a3509f614cbfd4943dc70d3f6d84e" "q-36227/selection/2928cadc70d5b702972833091abf1ef12b1231b3d41635f0fa3e5d01b4fa7d3c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-36227" :question/id #uuid "13799593-072b-539e-8ccf-ae779c80d604" :question/math-academy-id "q-36227" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{3}+{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{3}+{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3{x}^{2},2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t$ and $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨3{sin}^{2}⁡t,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3{sin}^{2}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =3\\cdot {(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}-4}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-36227/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-36227"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection/abca82202f39e74b29b0c30b93a13b7618c7c6e0de25e2a32f17f81920692125" :answer/id #uuid "7a36bf9b-f148-5f1e-a78e-1ee6546521b5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{12}{17}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" :answer/id #uuid "01a30646-a89f-587a-b37d-58b13ba4d78a" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{3}{10}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer/id #uuid "a65e60ea-c407-5752-9945-3aa2a546b5cb" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{5}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" :answer/id #uuid "50ee5d73-2ff7-5c6f-9133-6a1cd5834f01" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection/a417d5cd9e433598586b4612c651aa5fec144883f716e5fa4320b314d19105b0" :answer/id #uuid "dbc6d009-a76e-535b-8213-a52cf5da6fbf" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\frac{8}{13}" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "9d05aaa6-a0c7-5a98-8fd2-d4bdc2ee5ecb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94891/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" :answer-field/choices ["q-94891/selection/abca82202f39e74b29b0c30b93a13b7618c7c6e0de25e2a32f17f81920692125" "q-94891/selection/0417788a5409ad5447fb6d379532fc1bfef99c80e1411a914d6ff9762ba8f7ac" "q-94891/selection/edc901e84f0cdd5174afd49105ac3883566ea0e1e057217fb66083a56cc119ba" "q-94891/selection/523b37883474afade764165f552bc128b7640323ff7244275508d19e6f99b277" "q-94891/selection/a417d5cd9e433598586b4612c651aa5fec144883f716e5fa4320b314d19105b0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94891" :question/id #uuid "0df471af-1a79-5ea7-a599-084e8dac46d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-94891" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨t-1,3,2t⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2})),\\frac{∂}{∂z}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}))⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2x}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2y}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\frac{2z}{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x=t-1,$ $y=3$ and $z=2t$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨\\frac{2(t-1)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(3)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}},\\frac{2(2t)}{(t-1)^{2}+(3)^{2}+(2t)^{2}}⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),2t⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,0,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10},\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}⟩\\cdot ⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 1+\\frac{6}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 0+\\frac{4t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =\\frac{2t-2+8t}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{10t-2}{5{t}^{2}-2t+10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =\\frac{10(0)-2}{5(0)^{2}-2(0)+10} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{10} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["q-94891/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94891"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" :answer/id #uuid "a0ac3d6f-c22b-55f7-aa8f-d8cf3baa2894" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" :answer/id #uuid "927f2579-8c06-5c1e-8083-4de1953a2c67" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer/id #uuid "ed857ed8-cf10-5f8c-9ac3-45ab9d9fe8dd" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" :answer/id #uuid "2c24b928-4a6a-5b8e-8768-476e8acda203" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26" :answer/id #uuid "eb99fc72-be22-5643-a498-33078d197675" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "-1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "658ce548-a766-5a78-9925-28b8ffd124e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-94884/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" :answer-field/choices ["q-94884/selection/eadb4f132857861f1e64373661b9a01bc3625cc86bcffcc32659fc4899904fe0" "q-94884/selection/954f2b2ecf089f1c43903a74f4575cff67408acc792f8a71c271fa3076456a3b" "q-94884/selection/be9eb51cc2f690a7d5b4aa400428edabf5a5d631a176c92b7f40bdd47725ed03" "q-94884/selection/b13ac151352392d38026eed875b911708c50c896d6c09463410ac041030163ff" "q-94884/selection/e9bc05c525076ace5dedc0dc52c4f8497f441cd3bf97b666736f75acd9994b26"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-94884" :question/id #uuid "26023394-6b96-562c-97dd-06bdd04f7499" :question/math-academy-id "q-94884" :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}-{y}^{2}$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},2t-1⟩$ at the point $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $r(t)$ is given by $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))].$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))]=∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $∇f(x,y):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x={t}^{2}$ and $y=2t-1$ into the above to find $∇f(r(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2{t}^{2},-2(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $r(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t-1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,2⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2{t}^{2},2-4t⟩\\cdot ⟨2t,2⟩ \\\\  & =2{t}^{2}\\cdot 2t+(2-4t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =4{t}^{3}-8t+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $r(t)$ at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(0))] & =4(0)^{3}-8(0)+4 \\\\  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["q-94884/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ea1e5a55-a957-52cd-b693-0e48770672a6"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-94884"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3694"] :question/problem "If $f(x,y) = x^2 + 2 y^3$ and $\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin t \\ \\mathbf i + e^{t} \\ \\mathbf j,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}+2{y}^{3})\\,j \\\\  & =2x\\,i+6{y}^{2}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t$ and $y=e^{t}$  into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =2(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+6({e}^{t})^{2}\\,j \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,i+6{e}^{2t}\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+{e}^{t}\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\text{ }i+6{e}^{2t}\\text{ }j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\text{ }i+{e}^{t}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+6{e}^{2t}\\cdot {e}^{t} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t+6{e}^{3t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3695"] :question/problem "If $f(x,y,z) = xz\\ln{y}$ and $\\mathbf r (t) =  t^2 \\, \\mathbf i + t  \\, \\mathbf j +t^3  \\, \\mathbf k,$ compute $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r(t))].$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y,z){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(xz\\operatorname{ln}⁡y)\\,k \\\\  & =z\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,i+\\frac{xz}{y}\\,j+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡y\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x= t^2,$ $y = t$ and $z= t^3$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & ={t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\,j+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})\\,k \\\\  & =2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, applying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,i+{t}^{4}\\,j+{t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t\\,k)\\cdot (2t\\,i+\\,j+3{t}^{2}\\,k) \\\\  & =({t}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 2t+({t}^{4})\\cdot 1+({t}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)\\cdot 3{t}^{2} \\\\  & =2{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+{t}^{4}+3{t}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡t \\\\  & ={t}^{4}(5\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-8282"] :question/problem "Evaluate $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t}[f(\\mathbf r(t))]$ at $t = \\pi$ given that\n$\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x-1) \\, \\mathbf j$\nand\n$\\mathbf r (t) = \\sin{t} \\, \\mathbf i + \\cos{t} \\, \\mathbf j.$" :question/worked-solution "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf r'(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\nabla f(x,y) = (2y - 1) \\, \\mathbf i + (2x - 1) \\, \\mathbf j.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x = \\sin{t}$ and $y = \\cos{t}$ into $\\nabla f(x,y)$ to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,i+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)\\,j \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1\\,i+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\,j)\\cdot (\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\,i+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)\\,j) \\\\  & =(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1)\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t+(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-1)\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{cos}^{2}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2{sin}^{2}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =2({cos}^{2}⁡t-{sin}^{2}⁡t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t = \\pi,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\pi ))] & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi -\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi  \\\\  & =2(1)+0-(-1) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-3696"] :question/problem "Compute the rate of change of the function $f(x,y,z)=x^2-y^2-z$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\left \\langle \\sin t, \\cos t, t  \\right \\rangle$ at the point $t=\\dfrac \\pi 4.$" :question/worked-solution "The rate of change of $f(x,y,z)$ with respect to $t$ along the curve $\\mathbf r (t)$ is given by $\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] .$ To calculate this, we can use the chain rule.\n\nThe chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm d}{\\textrm d t} [f(\\mathbf r (t))] = \\nabla f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) \\cdot \\mathbf{r}'(t).\n$$\n\nFirst, we need to compute $\\nabla f(x,y){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =⟨\\frac{∂}{∂x}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂y}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z),\\frac{∂}{∂z}({x}^{2}-{y}^{2}-z)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2x,-2y,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute $x = \\sin t, y=\\cos t,$ and $z=t$ into the above to find $\\nabla f(\\mathbf r(t)){:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(r(t)) & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(t))] & =∇f(r(t))\\cdot {r}^{′}(t) \\\\  & =⟨2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,1⟩ \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\cdot (-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)-1\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1 \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of change of $f$ along the curve $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ at $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(r(\\frac{\\pi }{4}))] & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{4})-1 \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}-1 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
